<style is:global>@font-face{font-family:"Font Awesome 6 Free";font-style:normal;font-weight:900;font-display:swap;src:url(https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.5.1/webfonts/fa-solid-900.woff2) format('woff2')}@font-face{font-family:"Font Awesome 6 Brands";font-style:normal;font-weight:400;font-display:swap;src:url(https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.5.1/webfonts/fa-brands-400.woff2) format('woff2')}@font-face{font-family:"Font Awesome 6 Free";font-style:normal;font-weight:400;font-display:swap;src:url(https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/6.5.1/webfonts/fa-regular-400.woff2) format('woff2')}</style>
<section class="relative min-h-[55vh] flex items-center justify-center overflow-hidden bg-[#030a16]"><div class="absolute inset-0 z-0"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dwnnakrrh/image/upload/f_webp,q_auto:good,w_1600/v1782367505/eurocert/m14.png.webp" class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-30" alt="ISO 27017 Cloud Security"/><div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-[#030a16]/70 via-[#030a16]/40 to-[#030a16]"></div></div><div class="container mx-auto px-6 relative z-10 text-center py-20"><div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-1.5 rounded-full border border-eurogold-400/30 bg-eurogold-400/5 mb-6"><span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-eurogold-400 animate-pulse"></span><span class="heading-font text-[10px] text-eurogold-400 tracking-[0.3em] uppercase font-bold">Cloud security guidelines</span></div><h1 class="heading-font text-4xl sm:text-6xl md:text-7xl font-bold text-white mb-3 tracking-tight" style="text-shadow:0 0 30px rgba(212,168,67,0.3)">ISO 27017  -  Cloud Security</h1><p class="text-white/90 text-sm md:text-base max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">Guidelines for information security controls applicable to cloud services.</p></div></section><section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-white"><div class="container mx-auto px-6"><div class="grid lg:grid-cols-5 gap-10 items-start"><div class="lg:col-span-3 space-y-6"><div><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 tracking-[0.5em] uppercase mb-2 font-bold">Standard Overview</h4><h2 class="heading-font text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">ISO 27017  -  <span class="text-euro-600">Cloud Security</span></h2></div><p class="text-slate-500 text-[15px] leading-relaxed">To assess applicability, define organizational boundaries, the standard edition, services or activities, locations and intended assurance route before setting an audit scope.</p><div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-euro-100 bg-euro-50/40"><div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3"><div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-xl bg-euro-600 flex items-center justify-center"><i class="fas fa-info-circle text-white text-xs"></i></div><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">What is ISO 27017?</h4></div><p class="text-slate-500 text-sm leading-relaxed">ISO/IEC 27017 provides guidance on information-security controls for cloud services, including guidance for cloud service providers and customers. It supplements ISO/IEC 27002 control guidance with cloud-specific considerations. It is not a replacement for an information security management system or a standalone set of management-system requirements. Certification is usually considered in relation to an ISO/IEC 27001-based system and a defined cloud-services scope; confirm the applicable scheme before claiming a separate certificate.</p></div></div><div class="lg:col-span-2 relative rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl border border-slate-100 h-[320px] md:h-[400px] group"><img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dwnnakrrh/image/upload/f_webp,q_auto:good,w_1600/v1782367505/eurocert/m14.png.webp" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-700 group-hover:scale-105" alt="ISO 27017 Cloud Security"/><div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-slate-900/20 via-transparent to-transparent"></div></div></div></div></section><section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-[#f8fafc]"><div class="container mx-auto px-6"><div class="text-center mb-10"><h4 class="heading-font text-[11px] text-euro-600 tracking-[0.5em] uppercase mb-2 font-bold">Discuss the standard, cloud scope and applicable audit criteria for your organisation.</p><div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 justify-center"><a class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 heading-font text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[#030a16] bg-eurogold-400 rounded-full px-8 py-3.5 hover:bg-eurogold-300 transition-all shadow-lg shadow-eurogold-400/20" href="/contact/">Get Started <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-[9px]"></i></a><a href="tel:+919316012883" onclick="return gtag_report_conversion('tel:+919316012883');" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 heading-font text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-white border border-white/15 rounded-full px-8 py-3.5 hover:bg-white/5 transition-all"><i class="fas fa-phone text-[9px]"></i> Call Us Now</a></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="py-14 lg:py-18 bg-white" aria-labelledby="standard-details"><div class="container mx-auto px-6 max-w-5xl space-y-8"><h2 id="standard-details" class="heading-font text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">Applying ISO/IEC 27017 cloud information-security controls in practice</h2><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Who should consider it?</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Cloud service providers, SaaS and platform operators, infrastructure providers and organisations consuming cloud services can use the guidance to clarify security responsibilities. It is useful where provider and customer responsibilities are shared or service layers and subcontractors overlap. Define service models, regions, facilities, interfaces, customer responsibilities, supporting systems and third parties in scope. A customer should distinguish controls it operates from controls supplied by its cloud provider and understand how assurance evidence is obtained.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Key requirements</h3><ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed"><li>Establish an information-security governance and risk-management basis with scope identifying cloud services, roles, assets, customer interfaces and shared-responsibility boundaries.</li><li>Select relevant cloud security controls, including role clarity, access, asset handling, segregation, virtual-environment protection, monitoring and incident management as appropriate.</li><li>Address customer data protection, deletion and return, administrative access, change management, backup, logging and continuity through documented processes and contracts.</li><li>Assess suppliers and subcontractors, manage service changes and vulnerabilities, communicate security responsibilities to customers and retain evidence that controls operate as designed.</li><li>Review risks and control effectiveness through monitoring, internal audits, management review and corrective action. Control selection is risk-based; guidance does not mean every control applies identically to every deployment.</li></ul></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Certification and audit lifecycle</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Agree the audit criteria first: ISO/IEC 27017 is guidance, so an assessment may be an extension or additional control set within ISO/IEC 27001 certification, or another defined assurance engagement. For ISO/IEC 27001 management-system certification, stage 1 reviews scope, risk assessment, statement of applicability, system documentation and readiness. Stage 2 tests implementation and effectiveness, including cloud-control evidence, shared-responsibility arrangements and service operations. Findings are addressed before an independent decision. Surveillance and recertification follow the base scheme's cycle; the ISO/IEC 27017 control review is included only as agreed in scope. Timing depends on services, cloud models, regions, interfaces, workforce, suppliers and scheme rules; no fixed timing is promised.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Documents to prepare</h3><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Prepare information-security scope; cloud service catalogue and architecture; shared-responsibility matrices; risk assessments and control applicability records; statement of applicability where ISO/IEC 27001 applies; customer and supplier contracts; data-flow and location records; access, logging and change evidence; vulnerability and incident procedures; backup, continuity and deletion records; subcontractor reviews; training; internal audit; management review; and corrective actions. Protect sensitive architecture and customer information.</p></div><div><h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Frequently asked questions</h3><div class="space-y-4"><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Is ISO/IEC 27017 a standalone management-system standard?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">No. It provides cloud-security control guidance and is normally used alongside an information-security management system such as ISO/IEC 27001.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Does it apply only to cloud providers?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">No. It includes guidance relevant to cloud customers too. Each organisation should identify which controls it operates and which depend on another party.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Does it guarantee that a cloud service is secure?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">No. It supports control selection and assessment but cannot eliminate risk or guarantee security; assurance is limited to defined scope and sampled evidence.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Can I get a separate ISO/IEC 27017 certificate?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">Arrangements vary. Confirm whether the scheme assesses it as an ISO/IEC 27001 extension or under other defined criteria; do not assume standalone accreditation.</p></article><article><h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-900">What is a shared-responsibility matrix?</h4><p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">It records which security activities belong to provider, customer or another party for specified services, making boundaries and evidence expectations explicit.</p></article></div></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
